 nursing home reform  understaffing  is really the original sin of the  nursing home industry in that so many  other problems like neglect like  infection control really stemmed from it  and that was the case long before this  virus showed up there are no federal  regulations on staffing levels but  federally certified nursing homes are  required to have a licensed nurse on  duty CBS News found that roughly one in  fifteen US nursing homes was cited last  year for failing to meet standards for  sufficient nursing staff now the  outbreak which has sickened workers and  prompted others to stay at home is  hurting staffing levels even more is  that the conditions are far worse than  they ever have been april Verret is  president of SEIU Local 2015  California's largest union which  represents one quarter of the state's  nursing home workers Farah says the  median salary for nursing home workers  is about $23,000 a year other could say  operators need to increase pay and hire  more employees they also want the family  members to be allowed back if they've  been tested and have proper PPE for him  to recover or any other patient that's  in there or to stay well they have to  have their families there they  absolutely do and there's got to be a  way and we reached out to stony point  about the inspection report the facility  said we take such reports seriously and  this one was no exception and I quote we  took immediate corrective action upon  receiving the notice and instilled  additional training for all staff  members on proper infection control  procedures the facility says they have  an unwavering commitment to provide the  highest level of care Lisa Cooke who you  heard from there she says that should  include allowing families back into  nursing homes gail we feel for Lisa cook  Jonathan I see why she's mad and scared  we're pulling for Bruce and everybody  else in that situation thank you very  much
 

